feat(cli-sessions): execute Agent continuation through canonical owners

Support both continuation lifecycles exposed by the protocol without creating
parallel Session or message owners.

For replay-seeded child creation:
- resolve source context and exact cutoff before creating a Session row;
- route spawn, fork, execution-run, and legacy replay ingresses through one
  recipe builder and the canonical createSpawnedSession service;
- authenticate retry lineage, attach runners to the committed row, and settle
  orphaned rows once when launch fails;
- preserve generic native fork intent without silently degrading to replay.

For same-Session Agent transitions:
- inspect target support through the same decision used by mutation;
- require strict idle and confirmed source stop before committing the target
  current view and reserved transcript divider;
- admit the exact user input into durable Pending custody before activating the
  fresh target runtime;
- report partial or unknown effects honestly once the source has stopped.

Extract user-message admission from the ordinary send path so both send and
transition share encryption, provenance, idempotency, and Pending semantics.
Keep replay hydration failures distinct from a valid source with no dialog.

feat(ui-sync): orchestrate Agent continuation and fork strategies

Establish the UI data-plane owners for continuing work with another Agent,
either inside the current Session or through a replay-seeded child.

- gate Agent switching on the server capability and inspect target support
  before exposing a mutation;
- route same-Session transitions through the server-scoped machine RPC while
  preserving authorization, partial-effect, and retry semantics;
- carry typed source-context recipes through current spawn payloads and refuse
  compatibility paths that would silently strip required context;
- replace the implicit fork action with explicit Native and Replay strategies,
  including exact cutoff eligibility and replay configuration;
- keep resuming and armed-transition state in the Session store without
  redirecting ordinary composer sends;
- reuse the shared current-Agent projector for handoff identity rewrites and
  retire the catalog-local resume-key writer;
- preserve source context in temporary new-Session state and derive replay
  metadata through the existing canonical owners.

feat(ui-sessions): add guided Agent continuation flows

Give users an explicit, inspectable choice when continuing work with another
Agent instead of silently selecting a backend or replay path.

- share Agent picker option construction between new and existing Sessions;
- inspect same-Session transition support, arm the selected target in the
  composer, and make the submit button describe the pending continuation;
- keep the user's draft editable until transition effects begin and surface
  partial, unavailable, and retryable outcomes without losing input;
- present Native and Replay fork strategies before any fork effect from the
  header, info screen, or message actions;
- carry Replay source context into the new-Session composer as a removable,
  server-bound authoring chip;
- preserve machine, model, permission, and other authored options when source
  context is added or removed;
- expose selection state and loading semantics accessibly across picker rails;
- add localized continuation, strategy, divider, and source-context copy for
  every supported locale.

feat(ui-transcript): preserve historical Agent attribution

Render mixed-Agent Session history according to the Agent that produced each
sequenced row instead of reinterpreting the whole transcript through the
Session's current metadata.

- index validated transition dividers into ordered Agent-era boundaries;
- publish historical Agent identity to transcript rows and detached detail
  views without prop-drilling through every tool component;
- render transition events as dedicated separators naming both Agents;
- apply the producing Agent's unknown-tool, permission, and read-only policies
  to historical tool rows;
- keep unsequenced, malformed, unknown-Agent, and never-transitioned histories
  neutral rather than inventing attribution;
- prevent transition divider events from demanding user attention.
